      "figure_id": "Fig. 3",
      "caption": "Change in cellular cAMP level elicited by increased glycerol influx. (A) Proposed changes in glycerol metabolism, regulation, and gene expression in the presence of the GlpK mutant. Number 1 to 5 describe some of the key events taking place in the cell. (1) The missense mutation adjacent to the FBP-binding loop in GlpK renders the protein insensitive to FBP-inhibition, resulting in (2) increased glycerol influx with a concurrent increase in intracellular G3P. (3) Repression of adenylate cyclase mediated by elevated G3P leads to a decline in intracellular cAMP-CRP. (4) This change alleviates the cAMP-CRP-mediated repression of GlpR, a repressor of the glpFKX operon, leading to decreased glpK expression. (5) Increased influx of G3P results in the elevation of intracellular methylglyoxal, which may become toxic at a certain physiological threshold. Abbreviations (metabolites) – Glyc: glycerol, G3P: glycerol 3-phosphate, DHAP: glycerone phosphate, FBP: fructose 1,6-bisphosphate, F6P – fructose 6-phosphate, and MG – methylglyoxal. (B) Intracellular cAMP level in each single and double mutant and CY02 relative to wild-type. Error bars represent biological triplicates measured in technical duplicates. One-way ANOVA with Tuckey’s post hoc test was performed on cAMP concentrations of mutants relative to wild-type. All samples were tested significant (P-value < 0.001).",
